tor ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-138132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "14a251b8295654f53bb3e3a1b7ef561b3efb2a736f19545b21b1b6badaed7629"} {"question" "q-138132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-138132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-138132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-12-\\lambda  & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-12-\\lambda )(12-\\lambda )-256 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-400 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -20)(\\lambda +20) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =20,\\,-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "14a251b8295654f53bb3e3a1b7ef561b3efb2a736f19545b21b1b6badaed7629"} {"question" "q-170525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "17"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-179407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The maximum value of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4,$ is $12.$ Find a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-179407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n Notice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\n This is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\n the equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\n So, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\n we obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n \n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n As a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n Now, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $||y||=1,$ as usual.\n\n Since we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $12,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=12.\n$$\n\n Next, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=12:$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-12I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]-12[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Seeking a non-zero solution of $(B-12I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\n Finally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-179407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "14a251b8295654f53bb3e3a1b7ef561b3efb2a736f19545b21b1b6badaed7629"} {"question" "q-179407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-179407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-179407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=8{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $4{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{4},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -8 \\\\ -16 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -32 \\\\ -32 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}32-\\lambda  & -32 \\\\ -32 & -16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (32-\\lambda )(-16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda -1\\,536 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +32) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=48.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "14a251b8295654f53bb3e3a1b7ef561b3efb2a736f19545b21b1b6badaed7629"} {"question" "q-170526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "54"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "48"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+81{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -27 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(81-\\lambda )-729 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-90\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -90) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=90.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "14a251b8295654f53bb3e3a1b7ef561b3efb2a736f19545b21b1b6badaed7629"} {"question" "q-170515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "100"] ["math" "50"] ["math" "70"] ["math" "80"] ["math" "90"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170515" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "90"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $40.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $40,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=40.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=40:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-40I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}]-40[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-40I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "14a251b8295654f53bb3e3a1b7ef561b3efb2a736f19545b21b1b6badaed7629"} {"question" "q-170138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "q-170138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b6590b3eb9a5683d22e607ad4ea28e12a4a412ff4ffd0caa5b90d29e827a6bc"} {"question" "e-12663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-24x_1x_2+16x_2^2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $0.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is attained." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/example-12663.json" "file_sha256" "2ba1ec1ffaac2d8847158a07a5be44f7d5becc673ef201f233cb07fdb674b49b"} {"question" "e-12663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/613dd6029260150b75d9f037c947fbd3a3ab05cd63b74cb3ab29fdd4f2a0963c.png)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/dc259d743780ce58cf437ef11518a22a8eb551af9a934d2ad29bc418cdb98db7.png)  \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = 5,$ we get \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/a091f1b304062296158d63256cb19b2fe30b67340863b0f4355f9f0740d0bd71.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/example-12663.json" "file_sha256" "2ba1ec1ffaac2d8847158a07a5be44f7d5becc673ef201f233cb07fdb674b49b"} {"question" "e-12662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= -x_1^2+2x_2^2+6x_3^2+4x_1x_2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $6.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the maximum is attained." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/example-12662.json" "file_sha256" "358d09e6788e8223cefcd2a7757a794e5fcc98327566764557e29eeef851966d"} {"question" "e-12662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/3449b02c92b2b2534241d33f7b6288d8433eb907b5a97ad255aa9fd5022d5971.png)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/07bb866fbd14af52b5f3d01c91f6f58e1a7ef5bc4d70ada8c3a745035cae9e92.png)   \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is already normalized ($\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|=1$), we conclude that,   ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/467bac248fa9988f4ee41bb9adaaf5e7ecbdf0e7e47b66a091ff5edf799e6799.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/example-12662.json" "file_sha256" "358d09e6788e8223cefcd2a7757a794e5fcc98327566764557e29eeef851966d"} {"question" "e-12664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=16x_1^2+16x_1x_2+x_2^2$ given that $16x_1^2 + x_2^2=16,$ and find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is obtained." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/example-12664.json" "file_sha256" "7277b35500f4a90e9c975692a8cbfcd687f468d94cc9f57e1b143c625102ce26"} {"question" "e-12664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/fab48c5c9c4d9adee4f270c6138d0f69317cfa59ad9d6f5dab2d4cad15d368c6.png)\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_1^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{4^2}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$\\qquad$ $y_1={x_1} \\quad$ and $\\quad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{4},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/3042cf8eadb699a70b70daced51411fa570ec9cdf3b7b270943fe790739a5364.png) \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}= Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 32 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimise $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}16-\\lambda  & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )(16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-32\\lambda -768 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +16) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}} = -16.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=-16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-16I) & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-16I))\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/df1ad3d9484608d37e762f74d2a61776c8f82f8eb650dcb0a37f47c5037678c4.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/assets/c869aa1eb73c078eaac66201b1062b2d55a45d90ec2010b6af532ca601498a53.png) \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/example-12664.json" "file_sha256" "7277b35500f4a90e9c975692a8cbfcd687f468d94cc9f57e1b143c625102ce26"} {"question" "q-138085" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077353/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "db4e31c8e2d7086b77d2f23a6fe9cfe7d70c32ff5bb160a941e257a0514275d0" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "On the unit circle, Q=2(x1²+x2²)+(x1-x2)²=2+(x1-x2)². Thus its minimum is 2, attained when x1=x2. Original option 0 is unit length and satisfies this equality. The authentic solution derives the same vector. The history screenshot identifies submitted option 1 as the Incorrect answer. Stored and captured correct vectors have identical entries and normalization." "bindings" {"problem" "62b1cd07b9d7c9a7158a75ecd25b9bdbe1bd9742df8af6eb2bc5ba810eb83a9d" "worked_solution" "a17f7f0aefda282f1e30f0d63f2b70f7ed723d0a48883e612a306e757b76a93f" "original_field" "b79eb6e73db0c628cc801a8e8e7e083d54c6252449ce42579e154943e2090d6a" "reviewed_field" "e1dfee4f2795fd9ee8f9339cadaf5e68d45b21f1430ea70b2fcd2657bccf825a" "raw_widget" "83f797f97d8d74afcde8aadceb20bfc139a7df86e84eca7b9a9b63d4d37eac79"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-138085-before.png" "sha256" "5766670925d37edf1ca3cf8a12ecee3416a21d2af1c52e57fe669b630c88e71f"} {"path" "history-q-138085.png" "sha256" "ad3a6894de30a884962670